Hazard types
Every band (variable) of the hazard file that is used has a hazard type, set with the type entry of its [[hazard.variables]] entry in the settings file, e.g.:

water_depth: flood depth;
water_level: flood elevation (level), only for geometry exposure (method flood.level);
flood_duration: flood duration.

The methods of the linked vulnerability datasets determine which types are needed. A hazard file can therefore hold bands of several types, e.g. a depth band and a duration band for the method flood.depth_duration. In case of a risk calculation, every return period must have a band of every needed type; the return period of a band is set with the rp entry in the settings file (see risk maps).

Vulnerability file
The vulnerability curves must be given in a single NetCDF file, which is set via file under the [vulnerability] section of the settings file. It is recommended to place it in the vulnerability directory.






Important



The CSV vulnerability format (a water depth column followed by curve columns with #UNIT and #METHOD header rows) is no longer supported. Convert the curves to a NetCDF vulnerability dataset as described below.


The file can hold one or more vulnerability datasets. A vulnerability dataset is a NetCDF variable (named freely, e.g. default) that holds the damage fractions (values between 0 and 1) of a set of curves. A dataset &lt;name&gt; has:

One curve dimension, named curve_&lt;name&gt;. Its coordinate holds the curve ids (strings or integers). These are the ids the exposure data refers to (the fn_&lt;type&gt;_&lt;component&gt; columns of geometry exposure, the fn_curve of a grid exposure layer or the categories of a categorized grid layer).
One or more index dimensions, e.g. depth_&lt;name&gt; and duration_&lt;name&gt;. Their coordinates hold the hazard values (e.g. water depth) and must be strictly increasing. Each index dimension has a hazard type (e.g. water_depth or flood_duration), set with types in the settings file. The order of the index dimensions in the file is free; the axes are matched to the method by their type.
A method, naming the calculation method of the dataset (e.g. flood.depth, see Methods), set with method in the settings file.

A dataset with one index dimension holds 1D curves (e.g. depth-damage curves), a dataset with two index dimensions holds 2D curves (e.g. depth-duration-damage curves). An example of a file with a single 1D dataset:
dimensions:
    depth_default = 21 ;
    curve_default = 2 ;
variables:
    float depth_default(depth_default) ;
        depth_default:type = "water_depth" ;
    string curve_default(curve_default) ;
    float default(depth_default, curve_default) ;
        default:method = "flood.depth" ;
data:
    curve_default = "struct_1", "struct_2" ;
The curves are interpolated linearly (1D) or multilinearly (2D or more) between the given hazard values. Hazard values outside of the range of an index dimension get the value at the nearest end of that dimension (no extrapolation). A missing hazard value (nodata) gives a missing impact.
Water depths may be negative for assets that incur damage below the ground floor height, and the user is free to choose any water depth increments.






Important



Water depth units (e.g. feet or meters) must be consistent with the units of the flood hazard map and the exposure data (i.e., ground elevation, ground floor height).








Note



The aggregation of multiple hazard values per asset (formerly the #METHOD header row of the CSV file) is set per geometry file with zonal_method in its [exposure.variables] table, see the settings.
